    "evidence_text": "A mutation in Orai1 causes immune deficiency by abrogating CRAC channel function\n\nBoth approaches converged on a novel protein that we call Orai1, which contains four putative transmembrane segments.\n\nThis recruitment was dependent on the N-terminal domain of Nef encompassing its myristoylation.\n\nThe activation of T cells is the fundamental on switch for the adaptive immune system.\n\nCa2+ signaling is essential for T cell activation and starts as initial, short-lived, localized Ca2+ signals.\n\nHowever, the molecular composition of these junctions in T cells remains poorly understood.\n\nAccordingly, expression of this cytoplasmic fragment of JP4 inhibited SOCE.\n\nTen and 100 nm PTH induced maximum E4bp4 expression in MOBs.\n\nInhibition of receptor-mediated calcium influx in T cells by unsaturated non-esterified fatty acids.\n\nChem.\n\nHow closely related coexpressed transcription factors are differentially activated remains unclear.\n\nInstead, effective sustained physiological activation of NFAT4 is tightly linked to oscillations in nuclear Ca2+.\n\nThe nonreceptor tyrosine kinase Syk coupled CRAC channel opening to protein kinase C and ERK activation.",
